# Order Processing Microservices V1

Order Processing Microservices V1 is an event-driven backend system built with Java and Spring Boot. It handles order creation, payment processing, order status updates, and customer notifications using separate microservices.

The project uses RabbitMQ messaging for asynchronous service communication and Docker Compose to run all services, databases, and the message broker locally.

## Tech Stack

- Java 21
- Spring Boot
- Spring Data JPA
- MySQL
- RabbitMQ
- Docker Compose
- Maven
- REST APIs

## Services

- order-service
- payment-service
- notification-service

## Features

- Order creation and order lookup
- Payment processing with randomized SUCCESS or FAILED outcomes
- Order status updates based on payment result
- Custom notification generation based on payment success or failure
- RabbitMQ messaging for event-driven communication
- Separate MySQL database per service
- Shared RabbitMQ message broker
- Docker Compose setup for running the full system locally
- RESTful API structure using controllers, services, repositories, DTOs, and entities

## Event-Driven Flow

The system uses RabbitMQ events to coordinate communication between services.

```text
Create Order
→ OrderCreatedEvent published
→ payment-service consumes the event
→ payment-service creates payment with SUCCESS or FAILED status
→ PaymentCompletedEvent published
→ order-service consumes payment event
→ order status updates to CONFIRMED or PAYMENT_FAILED
→ notification-service consumes payment event
→ custom notification is created
```

## Project Highlight

**Event-Driven Microservices | Java, Spring Boot, RabbitMQ, MySQL, Docker Compose**

- Built separate order, payment, and notification microservices with independent databases.
- Implemented RabbitMQ messaging to trigger payment processing, update order statuses, and generate customized notifications.
- Containerized the full system with Docker Compose using MySQL databases and a shared RabbitMQ broker.

## Project Structure

```text
microservice-app
├── docker-compose.yml
├── order-service
│   ├── controller
│   ├── service
│   ├── repository
│   ├── entity
│   ├── dto
│   ├── event
│   ├── publisher
│   ├── consumer
│   └── config
├── payment-service
│   ├── controller
│   ├── service
│   ├── repository
│   ├── entity
│   ├── dto
│   ├── event
│   ├── publisher
│   ├── consumer
│   └── config
└── notification-service
    ├── controller
    ├── service
    ├── repository
    ├── entity
    ├── dto
    ├── event
    ├── consumer
    └── config
```

## API Overview

### Orders

```http
POST /api/orders
GET /api/orders
GET /api/orders/{orderId}
PATCH /api/orders/{orderId}/status
POST /api/orders/{orderId}/cancel
```

### Payments

```http
POST /api/payments
GET /api/payments/{paymentId}
GET /api/payments/order/{orderId}
PATCH /api/payments/{paymentId}/status
```

### Notifications

```http
POST /api/notifications
GET /api/notifications
GET /api/notifications/{notificationId}
POST /api/notifications/{notificationId}/markAsRead
POST /api/notifications/markAllAsRead


## Docker Services

Docker Compose starts the following containers:

```text
order-service
payment-service
notification-service
order-db
payment-db
notification-db
rabbitmq
```

## Local Ports

```text
order-service:        http://localhost:8081
payment-service:      http://localhost:8082
notification-service: http://localhost:8083
RabbitMQ UI:          http://localhost:15672
```

RabbitMQ default login:

```text
username: guest
password: guest
```

